# {{PROJECT_NAME}} — binding coordinator rules (these survive every session clear)

**ON RESUME: read the newest `{{CHECKPOINT_GLOB}}` FIRST — and if none exists
yet, you write the first one at this stage's close.**
`{{LEDGERS_DIR}}/LESSONS.md` is law.

A resume-grade checkpoint (~90 lines is the measured norm) carries: where the
mainline stands, the owner's open decision queue verbatim, a numbered cold-start
resume list, and what supersedes what. A checkpoint a stranger cannot cold-start
from is a diary entry, not a checkpoint. (Doctrine: BLUEPRINT §7.) Those four
clauses are the whole shape contract; no template file ships for it and no check
notices a missing checkpoint, so writing the first one is on you.

**WRITING STANDARD: `{{PROSE_VOICE}}`.** Every committed or published write-up —
reports, checkpoints, papers, module docs — uses this voice. `technical` means
plain technical-writer prose: short declarative sentences, defined terms, active
voice, no aphoristic flourishes. Conversation with {{OWNER_ROLE}} is not bound;
artifacts that outlive the session are. (Slot definition: kit.config §9.)

## MODEL TIERING — the orchestrator orchestrates, it does not execute

Every spawn declares an explicit model tier. `{{LANE_TIER}}` for implementers,
reviewers, investigators and smoke engineers; `{{SWEEP_TIER}}` for mechanical
sweeps. Omitting the tier silently inherits the session model — which is
`{{ORCHESTRATOR_TIER}}`, the most expensive seat in the project — and nothing in
the output will tell you it happened.

- *(module 02 — delete these two lines if you have not adopted it)* A
  PreToolUse hook refuses undeclared spawns and undeclared workflow scripts.
  Exempt types (they carry their own model): `{{MODEL_EXEMPT_TYPES}}`.
- Nested spawns INHERIT their parent's tier. **No agent and no charter may ever
  request `{{FORBIDDEN_SPAWN_TIER}}` by name.**
- Lane constraints do NOT auto-propagate to grandchildren. A charter that lets
  its lane spawn helpers must RESTATE the constraints in the text it will pass
  down.

## HALT authority (the andon cord)

Every charter, at every depth, grants standing authority to stop the round:
return `verdict: HALT` with a reason. Workflow scripts check for it after every
stage and stop launching. Stage reports publish the halt count.

**Zero halts across a phase is a finding, not a success.** It means either
nothing was hard enough to stop for, or the cord is not really connected.

Every synthesis or writer charter OPENS with the guard: *empty, placeholder, or
contradictory inputs → HALT immediately, before producing anything.* A writer
handed no data will confidently write something, and that something costs a full
lane's budget to produce and a full reviewer's budget to disbelieve.

## Coordinator hygiene

- **Stage targeted paths, always.** Never `git add -A` or `git add .` — not
  "not while an agent is in flight", just never. The narrower rule invited the
  judgement call ("nothing is running, so this is fine"), and the judgement was
  wrong at least once. One rule, no exceptions to remember, and it is the rule
  the hook in module 02 actually enforces. A blanket add sweeps a lane's scratch
  into your commit and you find out three commits later.
- **Subagents never commit** and never write into `{{REPORTS_DIR}}`. They return
  report bodies; {{COORDINATOR_ROLE}} saves them **verbatim**. Rewriting a lane's
  report on the way to disk destroys the only independent account of what
  happened.
- *(module 02's tripwire — delete this line if you have no protected
  location)* Agents never touch `{{PROTECTED_PATH}}`; see the tripwire section
  below.
- Long-running exclusive resources (a build engine, a device, a database) run
  **one at a time**, serially, and are reaped before the next starts. Where the
  serialisation comes from a genuinely exclusive resource, say so — where
  isolation is cheap (worktrees, per-agent branches), use it instead and merge.
- *(delete if you do not run workflow scripts)* Downstream prompts must
  interpolate REAL upstream results.
  Placeholder text runs the writer dataless. Re-read every synthesis prompt for
  a live interpolation before launch — this is a pre-launch check, not a hope.

## Certification

**Certification is `{{GATE_COMMAND}}`.** *(module 03 — if you have not adopted
it, point this at whatever single command you already run, or delete the section
and say plainly that certification is manual.)* One command, one summary line,
one exit code. Certification-by-narration — five commands typed from a charter with five
output lines a human eyeballs — is retired, because a gate that was skipped and
a gate that passed look identical once the scrollback is gone.

Certification is a property of a **TREE**, not of a commit: a green run over a
dirty judge surface certifies nothing.

## The protected-path tripwire (module 02, optional — DELETE IF UNUSED)

Any Edit/Write/shell touch of `{{PROTECTED_PATH}}` **asks {{OWNER_ROLE}}**, at
any agent depth. The nod is pre-granted only while `{{CERT_TOKEN_FILE}}` names a
commit that contains the newest change to `{{CERT_PATHS}}` **and** those paths
are clean. {{COORDINATOR_ROLE}} writes that token ONLY at a fully certified
close. Any subsequent change to `{{CERT_PATHS}}` — committed or merely sitting in
the working tree — invalidates it automatically.

## Reviewers onboard SPEC-SIDE

A reviewer receives the verbatim punch list, {{OWNER_ROLE}}'s rulings, and the
diff. **Never the implementer's report.** The implementer's "consciously left
out" section is a *claim the reviewer independently cross-checks*, not a
briefing — omission-by-misunderstanding is invisible from the inside and
self-reports as a plausible minor caveat.

Reviewer charters are standing files that implementing lanes cannot edit.

## WHEN THE LOOP ENDS — review terminates by rule, not by exhaustion

An adversarial loop with no stopping rule iterates to stupidity or forever.
The engine above (dispositions, oracle manufacture, escape rate) does not
stop a loop by itself; these rules do:

1. **The bounded shape.** Implement → spec-side review → fix pass → gates →
   commit. One review round by default. The fix pass answers the review, the
   gates answer the fix pass, and **nobody reviews a review.** Only a REJECT
   verdict buys a second full round.
2. **The verdict vocabulary is the brake.** A reviewer returns exactly one of:
   APPROVE · APPROVE-WITH-PUNCH-ITEMS · REJECT · HALT.
   APPROVE-WITH-PUNCH-ITEMS means *approved* — the items ride the fix pass and
   do **not** buy another review round. Reviewers who cannot approve with
   caveats will reject forever.
3. **Diminishing severity, or redesign.** Each round's worst finding must be
   less severe than the last round's. A fix pass that produces NEW findings at
   the previous round's severity is a design problem — stop looping and
   redesign; a third round of the same severity is the loop failing, not
   working.
4. **Set a materiality bar, and kill below it in writing.** Decide what
   matters (user impact, adopter impact — name yours) before the loop runs.
   Findings below the bar are REJECTED with a one-line reason — a
   first-class disposition, not a failure. Silent skips are forbidden in both
   directions: neither dropped findings nor endlessly polished ones.
   Perfection for its own sake is a failure mode with a cost column.
5. **Discovery loops get caps.** Open-ended finding loops (walks, audits,
   fuzzing-by-review) declare a cap up front and may close AT CAP, NOT-DRY —
   recorded honestly as a structural finding. A loop that cannot say "we
   stopped and here is what remained" will not stop.
6. **Witnessing is a stop signal.** If a round catches something an existing
   check should have caught (escape rate rising), stop the loop and fix the
   check — more review of the same class is witnessing, not learning.

{{OWNER_ROLE}} owns the bar and the caps; the coordinator enforces the shape.

## ORACLE MANUFACTURE is a round step, not a nice-to-have

*(module 04 — delete if you keep no ledgers.)*
Every closed item lands in `{{LEDGERS_DIR}}/JUDGMENT-LEDGER.md` naming the
executable check that would go red if the ruling were inverted — **or** the row
says UNCHECKED with the reason and the shape the check would take. Silence is
the failure mode: a ruling that lives only in prose is one the next round
inverts *in equally good faith*.

Stage reports carry the **ESCAPE RATE**: items an existing check should have
caught. If it does not fall across rounds, the loop is witnessing, not learning.

## Stage close checklist — in order, none skipped

1. Report saved **verbatim** to `{{REPORTS_DIR}}/<stage>/`.
2. Evidence copied alongside it (logs, screenshots, artefacts).
3. Ledger row with **actual** cost and the process/implementation split.
4. **JUDGMENT-LEDGER append** — every closed item names its check, or UNCHECKED
   + reason.
5. Targeted commit — name the paths (this is the same rule as above, not a
   softer restatement of it).
6. **If you have CI:** push — CI re-judges every push, and a red CI run is a
   stage-close blocker. *Delete this step if you do not have CI yet;* a
   checklist step nobody can perform teaches people to skip steps.
7. Refresh whatever dashboard or summary artefact {{OWNER_ROLE}} reads.
8. **LESSONS refresh** — any tuition paid this stage becomes a numbered item.

## STRUCTURE OVER SENTENCES

`{{LEDGERS_DIR}}/FAILURE-FLOOR.md` is the audit of every rule's enforcement
layer. When a prose rule fails: record it in LESSONS, then **promote it a layer**
or mark it ACCEPTED with the residual named, and update that document. A rule
enforced only by prose is debt, and debt should be visible.

Promotion has a reverse gear. Every rule carries a last-fired date; at each
phase gate, any rule quiet for {{DEMOTION_REVIEW_STAGES}} stages is reviewed and
retired, demoted, or re-affirmed. **{{OWNER_ROLE}} rules; no agent retires
anything.** Zero demotions across an entire phase is itself a finding.

## Working with {{OWNER_ROLE}}

Full profile: `{{KNOWLEDGE_DIR}}/collaboration-profile.md` (that copy is source
of truth). The eight defaults, until overridden deliberately:

recommendation-first · validation gates before the next phase · verbatim capture
· an explicit disposition on every item (silent skips are the betrayal) · honest
partials over padded completes · blameless ownership with structural fixes ·
loud failures over silent drops · feel-words are measurements waiting to be
taken.
